<Project>
  <!--
    A Directory.Build.targets here stops MSBuild's upward search, so the root one,
    which carries the development-time FFmpeg native copy (ADR-0014), has to be
    imported explicitly. Same shape as src/Directory.Build.targets.
  -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.targets', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <!--
    Wall-clock ratchet for tests (ADR-0072).

    Bans the APIs that make a test depend on how fast the machine is: sleeping,
    reading the wall clock, and starting a stopwatch. BannedSymbols.txt beside this
    file lists them, each with the replacement named in the message. RS0030 is an
    error, so a new wall-clock dependency fails the build rather than turning up
    months later as a flake on one CI leg.

    The shape is the public-API baseline's in src/Directory.Build.targets, for the
    same reason: a drift nobody has to notice is a drift nobody will. There, an
    undeclared public member fails the build. Here, an undeclared dependency on
    elapsed time does.

    On by default. A project turns it off by setting
    FrameFlowBanWallClockInTests to false in its own csproj, with a comment saying
    why. That makes every exception a visible line in a diff and the list of
    exceptions a grep. The list only gets shorter: a project comes off it when its
    last violation is gone, and a new test project starts on the right side of it
    without anyone deciding to put it there.

    In .targets rather than .props so the csproj's setting is what the condition
    reads. The same reasoning is written out in src/Directory.Build.targets.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(FrameFlowBanWallClockInTests)' != 'false'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);RS0030</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(FrameFlowBanWallClockInTests)' != 'false'">
    <PackageReference
      Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers"
      Version="3.*"
      PrivateAssets="All"
    />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)BannedSymbols.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The replacement comes with the ban. Every test project gets FakeTimeProvider,
    so the banned-API message can say "use FakeTimeProvider" and the package is
    already there when someone does. Unconditional: a project opted out of the ban
    is exactly the one that most needs a way off it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing" Version="9.*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A pool that does not have to grow, for the suites whose tests wait on a signal
    that arrives on a pool thread (#330).

    Such a test is deterministic in the sense that matters: it drives a
    FakeTimeProvider and waits for the work rather than for a duration, and its
    bound only catches a failure. What it still depends on is the runtime
    scheduling the continuation carrying that signal. Above the pool's minimum,
    threads are injected at a throttled rate rather than on demand, so where the
    ready threads are already busy a continuation can sit queued for seconds, the
    bound expires, and the test reports a defect in its subject that is not there.

    The minimum defaults to the processor count, small on a CI runner and large on
    a workstation, which is why this only shows up in CI: two tests in unrelated
    assemblies failed on consecutive `test (windows-latest)` runs of one commit
    while ubuntu passed both, and neither reproduced across three full local suite
    runs on 24 cores.

    A mitigation, not a proven fix. It removes injection delay as a candidate; it
    does not show injection delay was the cause. So the two suites also watch the
    task itself past the bound and report whether it was late or never coming,
    which is the part that will settle it.

    Opt-in, because the minimum is per process and `dotnet test` on the solution
    launches a host per assembly. Setting it everywhere would let twenty-odd hosts
    each grow to this many workers on a four-core runner, which is its own
    contention and would perturb the scheduling the suite exists to exercise. Only
    the assemblies that have actually failed this way opt in; widen it on evidence,
    not in advance.

    The number is the smallest that does the job rather than a comfortable one. What
    has to clear is the injection throttle, so the minimum only needs to exceed the
    pool threads these suites have blocked at once, which is a handful. Sixteen is
    four times a four-core runner's default and leaves far less headroom for the
    contention the mitigation is trying not to cause.

    In .targets for the same reason as the ban above: the csproj's setting is what
    the condition reads. Test hosts only, so it cannot mask starvation in shipped
    code.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(FrameFlowTimeBarrierTests)' == 'true'">
    <ThreadPoolMinThreads>16</ThreadPoolMinThreads>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
